# Phoenix playground

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:obsidian_livebook, path: "/home/scossar/projects/elixir/obsidian_livebook"},
  :phoenix_playground
])
```

## Demo

See: [Announcing Phoenix Playground](https://dashbit.co/blog/announcing-phoenix-playground)
GitHub repo: [https://github.com/phoenix-playground/phoenix_playground](https://github.com/phoenix-playground/phoenix_playground)

Phoenix Playground is for creating single-file Phoenix LiveView apps.

The announcement post pointed me to the [`Mix.install/2`](https://mix.hexdocs.pm/Mix.html#install/2) documentation. From the docs, it seems that dependencies can be installed by just specifying the dependency as an atom, e.g.:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
Mix.install([
  :phoenix_playground  # get the latest version
])
```

```elixir
defmodule DemoLive do
  use Phoenix.LiveView

  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
    {:ok, assign(socket, count: 0)}
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~H"""
    <span>Count: {@count}</span>
    <button phx-click="inc">+</button>
    <p>Live code reloading is supported. Although the browser window will
    need to be refreshed.</p>
    <style type="text/css">
      body {padding: 1em;}
    </style>
    """
  end

  def handle_event("inc", _params, socket) do
    {:noreply, assign(socket, count: socket.assigns.count + 1)}
  end
end
```

```elixir
PhoenixPlayground.start(live: DemoLive, port: 4001)
```

### Running ExUnit tests in a Livebook

Disable `ExUnit`'s run-on-exit and call `ExUnit.run/0` explicitly.

```elixir
Logger.configure(level: :info)
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)  # disable autorun

defmodule DemoLiveTest do
  use ExUnit.Case
  use PhoenixPlayground.Test, live: DemoLive

  test "it works" do
    {:ok, _view, _html} = live(build_conn(), "/")
  end

  test "the count increments" do
    {:ok, view, html} = live(build_conn(), "/")
    assert html =~ "Count: 0"
    assert render_click(view, :inc, %{}) =~ "Count: 1"
  end
end
```

NOTE: these tests can't be run while the server is running. Stop the server using the next section's cells if it's running.

```elixir
ExUnit.run([DemoLiveTest])
```

### Stopping Phoenix Playground

There's no `PhoenixPlayground.stop/0` function. My first approach at stopping the server was with:

```bash
lsof -i <port>

# e.g.
lsof -i 4001

# then:
kill -9 <PID>
```

As an alternative, first shut down the playground's supervision tree, including its web server. Then remove the saved child specification so that `PhoenixPlayground.start(...)` can be run again.

```elixir
# Shut down the playground's supervision tree
:ok = Supervisor.terminate_child(PhoenixPlayground.Application, PhoenixPlayground)
```

```elixir
# Remove the saved child specification
:ok = Supervisor.delete_child(PhoenixPlayground.Application, PhoenixPlayground)
```

